<section background-color="heading">
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item colspan="36" vertical-align="center">
      <p role="eyebrow">Teach it back</p>
      <h1>Your 20-second shield story</h1>
      <grid direction="vertical" spacing="none" divider="sm palette-5" marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-style="solid" marker-shape="circle" style="--marker-inline-size:30pt; --marker-gap:12pt;">
        <grid-item padding="8pt 0">
          <h4>Where?</h4>
          <p>In the stratosphere, above the weather.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="8pt 0">
          <h4>What does it do?</h4>
          <p>It absorbs much harmful UV; some still passes.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="8pt 0">
          <h4>What thinned it?</h4>
          <p>Long-lived human-made chemicals reduced ozone.</p>
        </grid-item>
        <grid-item padding="8pt 0">
          <h4>What plan is helping?</h4>
          <p>Countries are phasing those chemicals out together.</p>
        </grid-item>
      </grid>
      <p font-size="lg"><b>Finish with one accurate action—and one mix-up to avoid.</b></p>
    </grid-item>
    <grid-item colspan="24" padding="0">
      <ai-image background="true" image-style="atmosphere-cut-paper" aspect-ratio="portrait" prompt="Layered cut-paper editorial science illustration of a curious ten-year-old pointing toward an Earth cutaway at the lower right, the Earth in the same orientation as a curved cloud-white surface beneath a thin shield-blue atmospheric arc, UV-gold rays arriving from the upper left with some stopping in the arc and one continuing, a small action-green circle suggesting cooperation, deep-ink space, clean vector-like edges, subtle paper fibers, confident optimistic classroom tone, no text or lettering"/>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>